/**
 * codex-direct engine — OpenAI Codex as a first-party Open Session engine.
 *
 * Transport: the installed `codex` binary's app-server JSON-RPC surface, NOT
 * an SDK package. The reasoning is in codex-direct-protocol.ts's header; the
 * short version is that codex-usage.ts already runs this exact pattern in
 * production, the v2 protocol is a superset of any thin SDK wrapper (it has
 * `turn/steer`, `turn/interrupt`, typed `usageLimitExceeded`), and the schemas
 * are generated by the binary itself so a codex upgrade is checkable rather
 * than guessable.
 *
 * Containment / policy (the add-an-engine checklist):
 *  - Config gate, not an env flag: `directEngineEnabled("codex")`
 *    (engines-config.ts, `~/.opensession-engines.json`), read fresh per turn.
 *  - Kind gate copying `opencodeGateReason` semantics: deny by default on
 *    journal kind, kind-less runs refused, denials audited
 *    (`codex_direct_gate_denied`). The only escape is a module-scoped counter
 *    armed inside runCodexDirectSmokeTurn and matched to the private kind
 *    "codex-direct-smoke" — request/automation data can NAME that kind but
 *    can never arm the counter.
 *  - Auth: the shared codex-accounts pool via `pickOpenaiAccount` (same HRW
 *    affinity key convention as opencode, so a session keeps landing on the
 *    same account), seeded into an ISOLATED per-account CODEX_HOME —
 *    access-token-only with an invalid placeholder refresh, so this copy can
 *    never rotate the token family the live codex CLI logins depend on. See
 *    codex-direct-auth.ts. A usage-limit death calls `markCodexExhausted`
 *    (sideline state shared with opencode and pi) and flags
 *    `usageLimitExhausted` so agent-runner's fallback walk engages; a dry pool
 *    fails BEFORE any binary work, already flagged.
 *  - Tools: codex has no `canUseTool`, so enforcement is config-level.
 *    Ask mode gets a genuinely read-only sandbox (`readOnly`, no network) —
 *    stronger than a tool-name denylist, because it contains codex's built-in
 *    shell rather than removing it. Code mode gets `workspaceWrite` rooted at
 *    the session cwd. Denied and confirm tools are stripped from MCP servers
 *    through per-server `disabled_tools` (codex-direct-mcp.ts), and every
 *    sandbox-escape approval is screened: ask mode declines, unattended code
 *    mode runs the command through the org command policy
 *    (`bashAskPolicyReply`).
 *  - Subprocess env is minimal by construction (PATH/HOME/LANG/TERM + proxy
 *    and TLS passthrough + CODEX_HOME). No Open Session tokens. MCP servers
 *    receive their own credentials through the injected MCP config.
 *  - Journal: two-stage like the other in-process engines — a pre-engine
 *    record carrying the prompt, then an upgrade with the codex thread id in
 *    the journal's `claudeSessionId` slot (that field is the engine-session
 *    slot for every engine; the name is on-disk compat). NEVER a serverKey:
 *    the app-server child dies with the turn, so boot takes the continuation
 *    re-prompt path and `reattach` is null.
 *  - Transcript: store-only, the claude-direct recipe. `recordBksSessionFor`
 *    before any engine-keyed append; the user line is written early under the
 *    unified id with `opts.promptEntryId` as its uuid (so run-session's own
 *    early write upserts instead of duplicating), then re-appended keyed by
 *    the thread id. The codex rollout jsonl under the isolated CODEX_HOME is
 *    secondary evidence only — nothing reads it as the primary path.
 *  - Steer is REAL: `turn/steer` injects into the live turn, so steer()
 *    returns true while a turn id is live and false the moment it settles
 *    (callers then queue). The transcript user line for a steer is written at
 *    DELIVERY (codex's own userMessage item), never optimistically.
 *  - Exactly ONE terminal done/error per turn. A user cancel ends QUIETLY —
 *    the generator returns with no terminal event, so run-session records no
 *    failure for a run a human deliberately stopped.
 *  - Audit family `codex_direct_turn` (in/out, first-call-wins closer with a
 *    cancelled/abandoned finally backstop) plus `codex_direct_gate_denied`,
 *    field names matching `pi_turn`. Never raw prompt or reply text —
 *    `summarizeText` only.
 *
 * Documented v0 gaps:
 *  - No dial-oracle tool. codex 0.144.5's app-server has no client-side
 *    dynamic-tool registration (the `initialize` capabilities carry none, and
 *    `item/tool/call` dynamic tools come from plugin/app config), so an oracle
 *    would need either a codex plugin or a small stdio MCP entry point outside
 *    this module. Delegation still works through the opensession-sessions
 *    worker tools when the caller passes them in `inProcessMcp`.
 *  - `seedTranscriptEntries` is ignored (the handoff rides the prompt), same
 *    as pi and claude-direct.
 *  - Prompt images are passed as data-URL image inputs; codex accepts an image
 *    URL, but this specific shape has not been verified against a live turn.
 *  - api_key accounts are wired (see codex-direct-auth.ts) but unverified —
 *    there is no api_key account in the pool to test against.
 */

